<!DOCTYPE html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1.0"><title>Periodic Classification — Periodic Trends, s/p/d/f Blocks &amp; Properties | vlymbooq</title><meta name="description" content="Deep dive into Periodic Classification of Elements — Mendeleev's and Moseley's periodic tables, s, p, d, f blocks, periodic trends in ionization energy, electronegativity, atomic radius, and electron affinity."><style>@import url('https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Inter:wght@400;500;600;700;800;900&display=swap');*{margin:0;padding:0;box-sizing:border-box}:root{--bg:#09090b;--bg-card:#111113;--border:rgba(255,255,255,.06);--text:#fafafa;--text-sec:#a1a1aa;--text-muted:#52525b;--purple:#a78bfa;--emerald:#34d399;--red:#ef4444;--radius:12px}body{font-family:Inter,sans-serif;background:var(--bg);color:var(--text)}a{color:var(--purple);text-decoration:none}.nav{position:sticky;top:0;z-index:100;padding:14px 24px;background:rgba(9,9,11,.85);backdrop-filter:blur(16px);border-bottom:1px solid var(--border)}.nav-inner{max-width:900px;margin:0 auto;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between}.brand-text{font-weight:800;background:linear-gradient(135deg,#6366f1,var(--emerald));-webkit-background-clip:text;-webkit-text-fill-color:transparent}.nav-links a{padding:7px 14px;border-radius:100px;font-size:.82em;color:#a1a1aa}.container{max-width:900px;margin:0 auto;padding:24px}.lesson-body h2{font-size:1.5em;font-weight:900;margin:32px 0 12px}.lesson-body p{color:var(--text-sec);font-size:.92em;line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:12px}.btn{padding:8px 20px;border-radius:100px;font-weight:600;font-size:.82em;border:none;cursor:pointer}.btn-primary{background:rgba(167,139,250,.15);color:var(--purple)}.btn-success{background:rgba(52,211,153,.15);color:var(--emerald)}.story-card{background:linear-gradient(135deg,rgba(96,165,250,.06),rgba(167,139,250,.04));border:1px solid rgba(167,139,250,.15);border-radius:var(--radius);padding:20px;margin-bottom:20px;position:relative;overflow:hidden}.story-label{font-size:.72em;font-weight:600;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1.2px;color:var(--purple);margin-bottom:3px}.story-title{font-size:1.05em;font-weight:700;margin-bottom:8px;line-height:1.4;color:var(--text)}.story-text{font-size:.85em;line-height:1.7;color:var(--text-sec);margin-bottom:12px}.story-moral{font-size:.82em;font-style:italic;color:var(--emerald);padding:10px 14px;background:rgba(52,211,153,.06);border-radius:8px;border-left:3px solid var(--emerald)}.section-label{display:inline-block;font-size:.72em;font-weight:700;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1.5px;padding:4px 14px;border-radius:100px;margin:28px 0 4px}.part1{color:#60a5fa;background:rgba(96,165,250,.1)}.part2{color:var(--purple);background:rgba(167,139,250,.1)}.part3{color:var(--emerald);background:rgba(52,211,153,.1)}.part4{color:#f97316;background:rgba(249,115,22,.1)}.section-divider{height:1px;background:var(--border);margin:32px 0}.quote-box{background:linear-gradient(135deg,rgba(167,139,250,.08),rgba(52,211,153,.04));border:1px solid rgba(167,139,250,.15);border-radius:var(--radius);padding:20px;margin-bottom:20px}.quote{font-size:1em;font-weight:500;font-style:italic;color:var(--text);margin-bottom:6px}.attribution{font-size:.82em;color:var(--text-muted)}.q-card{background:var(--bg-card);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:var(--radius);padding:20px;margin-bottom:16px}.q-text{font-weight:600;font-size:.92em;margin-bottom:12px;line-height:1.5}.q-opts{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:8px}.q-opt{padding:10px 14px;border-radius:8px;border:1px solid var(--border);cursor:pointer;font-size:.85em;color:var(--text-sec);transition:all .2s}.q-opt:hover{background:rgba(167,139,250,.08);border-color:rgba(167,139,250,.3)}.q-opt.correct{background:rgba(52,211,153,.15);border-color:var(--emerald);color:var(--emerald)}.q-opt.wrong{background:rgba(239,68,68,.15);border-color:var(--red);color:var(--red)}.q-opt.disabled{pointer-events:none;opacity:.6}.q-soln{display:none;margin-top:12px;padding:12px;background:rgba(167,139,250,.06);border-radius:8px;font-size:.82em;color:var(--text-sec);line-height:1.6}.q-opt.correct~.q-soln,.q-opt.wrong~.q-soln{display:block}.q-soln.show{display:block}</style></head><body><nav class="nav"><div class="nav-inner"><a href="index.html"><span class="brand-text">vlymbooq</span></a><div class="nav-links"><a href="index.html">Home</a><a href="math.html">Math Map</a><a href="physics.html">Physics Map</a><a href="chemistry.html">Chemistry</a></div></div></nav><div class="container"><div class="lesson-body"><h2>Periodic Classification of Elements — Periodic Trends, s/p/d/f Blocks &amp; Properties</h2><p>The periodic table is the master chart of chemical elements, arranging them in order of increasing atomic number and grouping them by similar electronic configurations and chemical properties. <strong>Mendeleev's periodic law</strong>: properties of elements are a periodic function of their atomic masses. <strong>Moseley's modern periodic law</strong>: properties are a periodic function of atomic number. The long-form periodic table has 18 groups and 7 periods. Elements are classified into four blocks: <strong>s-block</strong> (groups 1–2, valence ns¹⁻²), <strong>p-block</strong> (groups 13–18, valence ns² np¹⁻⁶), <strong>d-block</strong> (groups 3–12, valence (n−1)d¹⁻¹⁰ ns¹⁻²), and <strong>f-block</strong> (lanthanides and actinides, valence (n−2)f¹⁻¹⁴). <strong>Periodic trends</strong>: atomic radius decreases across a period (increased nuclear charge) and increases down a group (more shells). <strong>Ionization energy</strong> — energy required to remove the outermost electron — increases across a period and decreases down a group. <strong>Electronegativity</strong> — the tendency of an atom to attract bonded electrons — increases across a period and decreases down a group. <strong>Electron affinity</strong> — energy released when an electron is added to a neutral atom — generally becomes more negative across a period and less negative down a group.</p><div class="quote-box"><div class="quote">"The periodic table is the chemist's map — a chart of the chemical elements that reveals the hidden patterns of matter."</div><div class="attribution">— Adapted from Primo Levi</div></div><div class="section-label part1">Part 1</div><h2>📜 Historical Development and the Modern Periodic Table</h2><div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Evolution</div><div class="story-title">From Döbereiner's Triads to Mendeleev's Table and Moseley's Modern Law</div><div class="story-text"><strong>Döbereiner's triads</strong> (1829): groups of three elements with similar properties where the atomic mass of the middle element is approximately the average of the other two (e.g., Li, Na, K; Ca, Sr, Ba). <strong>Newlands' law of octaves</strong> (1864): every eighth element has similar properties — analogous to musical octaves — but only worked up to calcium. <strong>Mendeleev's periodic law</strong> (1869): the properties of elements are a periodic function of their atomic masses. Mendeleev left gaps for undiscovered elements and correctly predicted their properties (e.g., eka-aluminium → Ga, eka-silicon → Ge). He reversed some pairs (e.g., Te and I) to keep them in the correct group — an inconsistency resolved later. <strong>Moseley's law</strong> (1913): the frequency of X-rays emitted from an element is proportional to the square of its atomic number (√ν = a(Z − b)). This established atomic number, not atomic mass, as the fundamental ordering principle — the <strong>modern periodic law</strong>. The <strong>long-form periodic table</strong> has 18 groups and 7 periods. The <strong>periodic law</strong>: the physical and chemical properties of elements are periodic functions of their atomic numbers. The position of an element in the periodic table determines its electronic configuration and chemical behavior — metals on the left, non-metals on the right, metalloids along the staircase (B, Si, Ge, As, Sb, Te).</div><div class="story-moral">The periodic table evolved from empirical observations (triads, octaves) through Mendeleev's predictive genius to Moseley's atomic-number-based foundation — now the organizing principle of all chemistry.</div></div><div class="section-label part2">Part 2</div><h2>🧬 Electronic Configuration and Block Classification</h2><div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Blocks</div><div class="story-title">s-block, p-block, d-block, f-block Elements — Configuration, Groups, and Periodicity</div><div class="story-text"><strong>s-block elements</strong> (groups 1 and 2): valence electrons in the ns orbital (ns¹⁻²). Group 1 (alkali metals): Li, Na, K, Rb, Cs, Fr — highly reactive, soft, low melting points, form +1 ions. Group 2 (alkaline earth metals): Be, Mg, Ca, Sr, Ba, Ra — less reactive than alkali metals, form +2 ions. <strong>p-block elements</strong> (groups 13–18): valence electrons in np orbitals (ns² np¹⁻⁶). Includes metals, metalloids, and non-metals. Group 18 (noble gases): completely filled ns² np⁶ — very low reactivity. <strong>d-block elements</strong> (groups 3–12): the <strong>transition metals</strong>, with valence in (n−1)d orbitals (1–10 electrons) and ns orbitals (1–2 electrons). They exhibit variable oxidation states, colored compounds, paramagnetism, and catalytic activity. <strong>f-block elements</strong>: <strong>lanthanides</strong> (Ce to Lu, 4f¹⁻¹⁴) and <strong>actinides</strong> (Th to Lr, 5f¹⁻¹⁴). Lanthanides show lanthanide contraction (steady decrease in ionic radius with increasing atomic number). <strong>Periods</strong>: Period 1 (2 elements, 1s), Period 2–3 (8 elements each, s + p), Period 4–5 (18 elements each, s + d + p), Period 6 (32 elements, s + f + d + p), Period 7 (incomplete). <strong>Periodicity</strong>: the repetition of similar properties after regular intervals due to similar valence electron configurations.</div><div class="story-moral">The s, p, d, f block classification directly reflects the filling order of atomic orbitals and explains the periodicity of chemical properties across the table.</div></div><div class="section-label part3">Part 3</div><h2>📊 Periodic Trends: Atomic Radius, Ionization Energy, and Electron Affinity</h2><div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Trends</div><div class="story-title">Variation of Atomic Radius, Ionic Radius, Ionization Enthalpy, and Electron Gain Enthalpy</div><div class="story-text"><strong>Atomic radius</strong>: half the distance between two bonded atoms. <strong>Trends</strong>: decreases across a period (nuclear charge increases, electrons added to same shell — stronger pull) and increases down a group (new shells added, shielding increases). <strong>Ionic radius</strong>: cations are smaller than their parent atoms (lost electrons → less repulsion, increased effective nuclear charge); anions are larger (added electrons → more repulsion). <strong>Ionization enthalpy (IE)</strong>: the minimum energy required to remove the most loosely bound electron from an isolated gaseous atom. First IE generally increases across a period and decreases down a group. Exceptions: IE of N > O (half-filled p³ stability) and IE of Be > B (fully filled s² is stable). Successive IEs increase sharply (IE₁ < IE₂ < IE₃) — the large jump indicates the number of valence electrons. <strong>Electron gain enthalpy (Δ_eg H)</strong>: the energy change when an electron is added to a neutral gaseous atom. Becomes more negative across a period (higher attraction). Group 17 (halogens) have the most negative values. Group 18 (noble gases) have large positive values due to stable octet. The second electron gain enthalpy is always positive (repulsion of already present negative charge). <strong>Trend anomalies</strong>: IE of Mg > Al (s² p¹ — Al has one electron in a higher-energy p orbital, easier to remove).</div><div class="story-moral">Atomic size decreases across a period and increases down a group; ionization energy and electron affinity follow predictable trends with exceptions explained by orbital stability (half-filled, fully filled).</div></div><div class="section-label part4">Part 4</div><h2>⚖️ Electronegativity, Valence, and Periodic Applications</h2><div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Bonding</div><div class="story-title">Electronegativity Scales, Oxidation States, Periodic Applications, and Anomalies</div><div class="story-text"><strong>Electronegativity (χ)</strong>: the tendency of an atom in a molecule to attract shared electrons toward itself. The <strong>Pauling scale</strong> (most common, χ_F = 4.0): χ_A − χ_B = 0.102 √Δ, where Δ is the extra bond energy in kcal/mol. The <strong>Mulliken scale</strong>: χ = (IE + EA)/2 (average of ionization energy and electron affinity). The <strong>Allred-Rochow scale</strong>: based on effective nuclear charge and covalent radius. Trend: electronegativity increases across a period and decreases down a group. F is the most electronegative element (4.0), Cs and Fr are the least (~0.7). <strong>Oxidation states</strong>: the number of electrons an atom uses for bonding. s-block: +1 (group 1), +2 (group 2). p-block: variable — group 13 (+3), group 14 (±4), group 15 (−3 to +5), group 16 (−2 to +6), group 17 (−1 to +7). d-block: variable (Fe²⁺/Fe³⁺, Cu⁺/Cu²⁺, Mn²⁺ to Mn⁷⁺). <strong>Periodic applications</strong>: predicting bond type (ionic if Δχ > 1.7, covalent if Δχ < 1.7), explaining chemical reactivity (alkali metals are strong reductants, halogens are strong oxidants), understanding anomalous properties of second-period elements (Li differs from other group 1 due to small size), and the <strong>diagonal relationship</strong> (Li with Mg, Be with Al, B with Si — due to similar charge/radius ratio). <strong>Inert pair effect</strong>: the reluctance of s-electrons in heavier p-block elements to participate in bonding (e.g., Tl⁺ more stable than Tl³⁺, Pb²⁺ more stable than Pb⁴⁺).</div><div class="story-moral">Electronegativity determines bond polarity and chemical reactivity; periodic anomalies like the inert pair effect and diagonal relationship reveal nuances in periodicity.</div></div><div class="section-divider"></div><h2 style="margin-top:0">🎯 Practice</h2><div id="practice-qs"></div><div style="margin-top:16px"><button class="btn btn-primary" onclick="resetPractice()">🔄 Reset All</button><a href="chemistry.html" class="btn btn-success">← Back to Chemistry</a></div></div></div><script>var practiceQs=[{id:46109,text:"📜 Moseley's law established that the fundamental ordering property of elements is:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Atomic number",c:true},{l:"B",t:"Atomic mass",c:false},{l:"C",t:"Atomic radius",c:false},{l:"D",t:"Density",c:false}],sol:"<strong>Moseley's law</strong> (√ν = a(Z − b)) showed that atomic number Z, not atomic mass, determines element identity and ordering in the periodic table."},{id:46110,text:"📜 Mendeleev left gaps in his periodic table to:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Predict undiscovered elements",c:true},{l:"B",t:"Balance the table layout",c:false},{l:"C",t:"Separate metals from non-metals",c:false},{l:"D",t:"Account for isotopes",c:false}],sol:"Mendeleev left <strong>gaps</strong> for unknown elements and successfully predicted their properties — e.g., eka-aluminium (Ga) and eka-silicon (Ge)."},{id:46111,text:"📜 The modern periodic law states that the properties of elements are a periodic function of:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Atomic number",c:true},{l:"B",t:"Atomic mass",c:false},{l:"C",t:"Atomic volume",c:false},{l:"D",t:"Number of neutrons",c:false}],sol:"The <strong>modern periodic law</strong>: physical and chemical properties of elements are periodic functions of their <strong>atomic numbers</strong>."},{id:46112,text:"🧬 d-block elements are characterized by the filling of which orbitals?",opts:[{l:"A",t:"(n−1)d orbitals",c:true},{l:"B",t:"ns orbitals",c:false},{l:"C",t:"np orbitals",c:false},{l:"D",t:"(n−2)f orbitals",c:false}],sol:"<strong>d-block elements</strong> (transition metals) have valence electrons in the (n−1)d orbitals (groups 3–12)."},{id:46113,text:"🧬 Lanthanide contraction refers to:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Decrease in ionic radius across the lanthanide series",c:true},{l:"B",t:"Increase in atomic radius across lanthanides",c:false},{l:"C",t:"Decrease in atomic number across lanthanides",c:false},{l:"D",t:"Decrease in ionization energy across lanthanides",c:false}],sol:"<strong>Lanthanide contraction</strong>: the steady decrease in ionic radius across the lanthanide series (Ce to Lu) due to poor shielding by 4f electrons."},{id:46114,text:"🧬 How many groups does the long-form periodic table have?",opts:[{l:"A",t:"18",c:true},{l:"B",t:"8",c:false},{l:"C",t:"16",c:false},{l:"D",t:"21",c:false}],sol:"The <strong>long-form periodic table</strong> has 18 groups and 7 periods, with the s, p, d, f blocks."},{id:46115,text:"📊 Atomic radius generally:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Decreases across a period and increases down a group",c:true},{l:"B",t:"Increases across a period and decreases down a group",c:false},{l:"C",t:"Decreases both across a period and down a group",c:false},{l:"D",t:"Increases both across a period and down a group",c:false}],sol:"<strong>Atomic radius</strong> decreases across a period (increased Z_eff pulls electrons tighter) and increases down a group (more shells, increased shielding)."},{id:46116,text:"📊 The first ionization energy of nitrogen is higher than oxygen because:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"N has a half-filled 2p³ configuration which is extra stable",c:true},{l:"B",t:"N has a smaller atomic radius",c:false},{l:"C",t:"O has more protons",c:false},{l:"D",t:"O has paired electrons in 2p⁴",c:false}],sol:"The <strong>half-filled p³ configuration</strong> of N (2p³) has extra stability due to exchange energy, making it harder to remove an electron compared to O (2p⁴)."},{id:46117,text:"📊 Electron gain enthalpy of halogens is:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Highly negative",c:true},{l:"B",t:"Highly positive",c:false},{l:"C",t:"Zero",c:false},{l:"D",t:"Slightly positive",c:false}],sol:"<strong>Halogens</strong> (group 17) have the most negative electron gain enthalpies because adding one electron completes their octet, releasing substantial energy."},{id:46118,text:"⚖️ On the Pauling scale, the most electronegative element is:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Fluorine (4.0)",c:true},{l:"B",t:"Oxygen (3.5)",c:false},{l:"C",t:"Chlorine (3.0)",c:false},{l:"D",t:"Nitrogen (3.0)",c:false}],sol:"<strong>Fluorine</strong> has the highest electronegativity (χ = 4.0 on the Pauling scale), being the strongest electron attractor."},{id:46119,text:"⚖️ The inert pair effect refers to:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Reluctance of s-electrons in heavier p-block elements to participate in bonding",c:true},{l:"B",t:"Stability of noble gases",c:false},{l:"C",t:"Pairing of electrons in d-orbitals",c:false},{l:"D",t:"Formation of inert complexes",c:false}],sol:"The <strong>inert pair effect</strong>: in heavier p-block elements (Tl, Pb, Bi), the ns² electrons are reluctant to bond, making lower oxidation states more stable (Tl⁺, Pb²⁺, Bi³⁺)."},{id:46120,text:"⚖️ The diagonal relationship in the periodic table is observed between:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Li and Mg",c:true},{l:"B",t:"Li and Na",c:false},{l:"C",t:"C and Si",c:false},{l:"D",t:"F and Cl",c:false}],sol:"The <strong>diagonal relationship</strong> exists between Li and Mg (also Be and Al, B and Si) due to similar charge/radius ratio, giving similar properties."}];var answered={},correctCount=0;function renderPractice(){var c=document.getElementById('practice-qs'),h='';for(var i=0;i<practiceQs.length;i++){var q=practiceQs[i],o='';for(var j=0;j<q.opts.length;j++){o+='<div class="q-opt" onclick="selectOpt('+q.id+','+j+')">'+q.opts[j].l+'. '+q.opts[j].t+'</div>';}h+='<div class="q-card" id="q-'+q.id+'"><div class="q-text">'+(i+1)+'. '+q.text+'</div><div class="q-opts">'+o+'</div><div class="q-soln" id="soln-'+q.id+'"><strong>'+getCorrectLabel(q)+'</strong><br>'+q.sol+'</div></div>';}c.innerHTML=h;}function getCorrectLabel(q){for(var i=0;i<q.opts.length;i++){if(q.opts[i].c)return q.opts[i].l+'. '+q.opts[i].t;}return '';}function selectOpt(qId,idx){if(answered[qId])return;answered[qId]=true;var q=practiceQs.filter(function(x){return x.id===qId})[0];var opts=document.querySelectorAll('#q-'+qId+' .q-opt'),isCorrect=q.opts[idx].c;for(var i=0;i<opts.length;i++)opts[i].classList.add('disabled');if(isCorrect){opts[idx].classList.add('correct');correctCount++;}else{opts[idx].classList.add('wrong');for(var i=0;i<q.opts.length;i++){if(q.opts[i].c)opts[i].classList.add('correct');}}document.getElementById('soln-'+qId).classList.add('show');}function resetPractice(){if(!confirm('Reset?'))return;answered={};correctCount=0;document.querySelectorAll('.q-opt').forEach(function(e){e.className='q-opt'});document.querySelectorAll('.q-soln').forEach(function(e){e.classList.remove('show')});renderPractice();}renderPractice();</script></body></html>